        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Does a Surge Protector Do? It Diverts Spikes, It Does Not Keep the Power On
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surge protector sits between the wall outlet and your electronics and diverts brief voltage spikes away from them. Standard US outlets deliver about 120 volts. A surge is a spike above that, lasting a tiny fraction of a second, and it can come from a lightning strike nearby, utility switching, or large appliances such as air conditioners cycling on and off inside your own home. Small, repeated surges are far more common than lightning, and they slowly wear down the power supplies in TVs, computers and chargers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How It Works: Metal Oxide Varistors</h3>

          <p>
            Inside almost every plug-in surge protector are metal oxide varistors, usually called MOVs. At normal voltage they do nothing. When voltage climbs past a threshold, they conduct and shunt the excess to the ground or neutral wire. Each surge they absorb wears them down a little, which is why surge protectors do not last forever and why the joule rating, covered below, is really a measure of how much wear the strip can take.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surge Protector vs Power Strip</h3>

          <p>
            A power strip and a surge protector look identical, but a basic power strip is just an extension cord with extra outlets and a circuit breaker. It offers no surge protection at all. The packaging is the giveaway: a real surge protector lists a joule rating, a clamping voltage or voltage protection rating, and UL 1449 certification. If none of those are printed, assume it is a plain power strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plug-In vs Whole-House Surge Protection</h3>

          <p>
            This guide covers plug-in surge protectors, the strips you put behind a TV or under a desk. A whole-house unit is installed at the electrical panel and stops large surges coming in from the utility line before they reach any circuit. It cannot stop surges generated inside the house, and it does not clamp as low as a good strip. The two work best together, which is why electricians recommend layering them. If you want panel-level protection, our guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole house surge protectors</a> covers Type 1 and Type 2 units and what installation involves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Joules, Clamping Voltage and UL 1449: The Three Numbers That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every surge protector box is covered in numbers, but three decide whether it is any good. Learn these and you can judge any strip in a few seconds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joule Rating: How Much It Can Absorb</h3>

          <p>
            The joule rating is the total energy the protector can absorb before its MOVs are worn out. Higher is better, because a 4,000 joule strip can soak up more surges, or bigger ones, before it stops protecting. It is not a measure of how well it protects on any one surge, though. Think of it as a fuel tank rather than a speedometer. Our picks range from 1,080 joules for a desk unit to 4,320 joules for a home theater strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clamping Voltage: When It Starts Working</h3>

          <p>
            Clamping voltage is the level at which the protector starts diverting energy. UL tests this and publishes it as the Voltage Protection Rating, or VPR, in steps such as 330, 400 and 500 volts. Lower is better, because the strip lets less of the spike through to your gear. A 330 volt or 400 volt VPR is what to look for on electronics. Many Amazon listings quote joules but leave the VPR off, so check the label on the strip or the manufacturer's spec sheet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UL 1449: The Certification That Proves It</h3>

          <p>
            UL 1449 is the safety standard for surge protective devices. A strip certified or tested to UL 1449 has had its surge components evaluated, not just its wiring. UL 1363 covers the power strip itself. Look for UL 1449 specifically, from UL or from another recognised lab such as ETL that tests to the same standard. A product that only says UL listed may have been tested as a power strip and nothing more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Response Time and Noise Filtering</h3>

          <p>
            Response time claims of one nanosecond or less appear on almost every box and tell you little, because MOVs all react quickly. EMI and RFI noise filtering is a nice extra for audio gear, where it can reduce hum, but it does not protect against surges. Treat both as secondary to the three numbers above.
          </p>

