        <div id="joules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many Joules Do You Need? About 1,000 for Chargers, 2,500 or More for a TV or PC
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the joule rating to what the strip protects. More is never harmful, but you do not need a home theater strip for a phone charger.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Phones, Lamps and Small Chargers: 600 to 1,000 Joules</h3>

          <p>
            Low-cost devices with simple power supplies do not need much. A desk unit like the APC PE6U4 at 1,080 joules is plenty for chargers, a lamp and a laptop brick.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Computers, Monitors and Routers: 1,000 to 2,500 Joules</h3>

          <p>
            A home office with a computer, monitor, printer and networking gear benefits from a mid-range strip. The Anker 351 at 2,100 joules sits here, and the Belkin USB-C strip at 3,480 joules gives extra headroom for a desk that also hosts a NAS or external drives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">TVs, Gaming PCs and Home Theater: 2,500 Joules and Up</h3>

          <p>
            Large TVs, AV receivers, gaming consoles and desktop PCs with expensive graphics cards are where a high rating pays off. Pick a strip of at least 2,500 joules, and preferably one that also protects the coax and Ethernet lines feeding the setup, such as the Belkin 4,320 joule strip or the Tripp Lite Isobar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indicator-lights" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Protected and Grounded Lights: What Each One Tells You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most good surge protectors have one or two small LEDs near the switch. They are the only way to know, without opening the strip, whether it is still doing its job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Protected Light</h3>

          <p>
            A protected light stays on while the surge components are working. When the MOVs have absorbed their limit, the light goes out. Some strips, including many from Tripp Lite and APC, then cut power to the outlets entirely so you notice; others keep supplying power with no protection, which is why it is worth glancing at the light now and then. If the protected light is off, replace the strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Grounded Light</h3>

          <p>
            A grounded light confirms that the wall outlet is properly grounded. A surge protector diverts much of the excess energy to the ground wire, so on an ungrounded outlet it cannot protect fully. If the grounded light stays off, the problem is the outlet, not the strip. Older homes with two-prong outlets, or three-prong outlets that were swapped in without a ground wire, are the usual cause. An electrician can fix it, or you can move the strip to a grounded outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Lights at All</h3>

          <p>
            A strip with only a lit power switch and no protection indicator gives you no way to tell when it has worn out. That is reason enough to skip it for anything more valuable than a lamp.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usb-c" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. USB-C Ports on a Surge Protector: Handy, but Check the Wattage
          </h2>

          <p>
            USB ports on a surge protector save you a charger brick and an outlet, and they share in the strip's protection. The catch is that most of them are slow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Watts, Not Just the Port Shape</h3>

          <p>
            A USB-C port can deliver anything from 15 watts to over 100 watts depending on what is behind it. The Anker 351 lists a 20W USB-C port, enough to fast-charge a current iPhone. The Tripp Lite TLP118DBUC shares 4 amps between its USB-C and USB-A ports, which suits phones and tablets. None of the strips here is designed to charge a laptop at full speed, so keep your laptop's own charger plugged into an AC outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shared Output</h3>

          <p>
            USB ports on a strip almost always share one power budget. Plug in two phones and each charges more slowly than one would alone. The Anker listing, for example, caps total USB output at 15W when several ports are in use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Skip USB Entirely</h3>

          <p>
            Behind a TV or AV rack, USB ports go unused and add nothing. There a strip with more joules and coax protection, like the Belkin 4,320 joule model or the Isobar, is the better use of the space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When to Replace a Surge Protector: After a Big Hit or Every Three to Five Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Surge protectors wear out, and most people never replace theirs. Because the MOVs degrade with every surge they absorb, a ten-year-old strip may be little more than a power strip by now.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Replace It Immediately If</h3>

          <p>
            The protected light is off, the strip took a nearby lightning strike or a major utility surge, it shows scorch marks, smells of burnt plastic, feels hot, or the outlets have gone loose. Any of these means the protection is gone or the strip has become a hazard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Replace It on a Schedule</h3>

          <p>
            Without an obvious event, a common rule of thumb is every three to five years, sooner in areas with frequent storms or unstable power. Since you cannot see how much life is left in the MOVs, a strip that protects an expensive TV or computer is worth replacing on the early side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Warranty Before You Throw It Out</h3>

          <p>
            Many brands, including Tripp Lite, Belkin, APC and Anker, offer a connected equipment warranty that covers devices damaged while plugged into a working strip. If a surge damaged your gear, keep the strip and the receipt and contact the manufacturer before discarding anything. Each warranty has its own claim process and limits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-outages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Surge Protectors and Power Outages: The Danger Is When the Power Comes Back
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surge protector does nothing while the power is out, but the moment power returns is one of the riskiest for electronics. When the grid comes back on after a storm, voltage can spike and fluctuate for a few seconds as every appliance on the street starts at once.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Unplug or Switch Off During Long Outages</h3>

          <p>
            If the power goes out for more than a few minutes, switch off the surge protector or unplug sensitive electronics. Turn them back on a few minutes after power is restored, once the voltage has settled. This is free and protects even the oldest strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running Electronics on a Generator</h3>

          <p>
            Portable generators, especially conventional open-frame models, can produce voltage swings and electrical noise. A surge protector between a generator extension cord and a TV or computer is a sensible buffer, though an inverter generator produces cleaner power in the first place. Our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> explains which types are safe for sensitive electronics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surge Protector vs UPS</h3>

          <p>
            A surge protector diverts spikes but cuts out the instant power fails. A UPS, or uninterruptible power supply, adds a battery that keeps a computer, modem or router running through short outages and gives you time to shut down safely, and most include surge-protected outlets too. If losing work or internet during a blink is the bigger problem, see our picks for the <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">best UPS battery backup</a>. For the bigger version of that idea, our explainer on a <a href="/whole-house-ups/">whole house UPS</a> covers home batteries that switch the entire panel over in milliseconds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Where to Use a Surge Protector, and Where Not To
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surge protector belongs in front of anything with a circuit board. A few appliances should never be plugged into one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Always Protect</h3>

          <p>
            TVs, computers, monitors, game consoles, AV receivers, routers and modems, smart home hubs, printers and phone chargers. Also consider appliances with electronic controls, such as a microwave or a coffee maker with a digital display, if an outlet is free.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Plug In</h3>

          <p>
            Space heaters, portable air conditioners, refrigerators, freezers and other high-draw appliances should go straight into a wall outlet. They can overload a strip, and heater manufacturers and fire safety agencies warn against using power strips with them. Large shop tools such as table saws and air compressors belong on the wall outlet too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Daisy-Chain</h3>

          <p>
            Plugging one surge protector into another, or into an extension cord, is a fire risk and can void the connected equipment warranty. If you run out of outlets, buy a strip with more of them, like one of the 12-outlet models above.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Other Lines Too</h3>

          <p>
            Surges can arrive through cable TV coax, phone lines and Ethernet as well as the power cord. A strip with coax and Ethernet jacks, like the Isobar, closes those back doors for a home theater or office.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Power Strip Instead of a Surge Protector</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many cheap strips look identical to surge protectors but have no surge components at all. If the box does not list a joule rating, a voltage protection rating and UL 1449, it is an extension cord with extra outlets. Check the label before you plug an expensive TV or computer into it, and replace any unlabelled strips you already own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping by Joules and Ignoring the Clamping Voltage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A huge joule number tells you how long a strip will last, not how much of a spike reaches your gear. A strip with fewer joules and a 330 volt rating can protect better on each surge than one with more joules and a 500 volt rating. Read both numbers, and favour UL 1449 certified strips that publish their voltage protection rating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the Same Strip for a Decade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Surge protectors wear out with every spike they absorb, and many keep passing power after their protection is gone. A strip that has sat behind the TV since the last one was bought is probably not protecting much. Check the protected light, replace after any major surge, and plan on a new strip every three to five years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Grounded Light</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A surge protector needs a properly grounded outlet to divert energy safely. If the grounded light stays off, the strip cannot protect fully, however many joules it has. Move it to a grounded outlet or have an electrician fix the wiring rather than assuming the strip is faulty or ignoring the warning altogether.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging Space Heaters and Fridges Into a Strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-draw appliances can overload a surge protector, overheat it, and in the worst cases start a fire. Space heaters, refrigerators, portable air conditioners and large tools belong in a wall outlet. Keep the strip for electronics, and never plug one surge protector into another to get more outlets.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

