        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best surge protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Tripp Lite TLP118DBUC is the best surge protector for most homes. Its listing gives it 11 outlets, a 4,200 joule rating, UL 1363 and UL 1449 certification, a USB-C port and an 8 foot flat-plug cord. For a TV with cable, the Belkin 4,320 joule strip adds coax protection, and the Anker 351 is the best pick for fast phone charging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many joules should a surge protector have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 1,000 joules is enough for phone chargers, lamps and small electronics. Computers, monitors and routers are better served by 1,000 to 2,500 joules. For a large TV, home theater or gaming PC, choose at least 2,500 joules, and ideally a strip with coax and Ethernet protection too. More joules means the strip can absorb more surges before wearing out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good clamping voltage for a surge protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a UL 1449 voltage protection rating of 330 or 400 volts for electronics. Lower is better, because less of the spike passes through to your devices. Ratings of 500 volts or higher still offer protection but let more through. Many Amazon listings quote joules but not the clamping voltage, so check the strip's label or spec sheet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do surge protectors last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common rule of thumb is three to five years, but it depends on how many surges the strip absorbs. One large surge, such as a nearby lightning strike, can use up its protection in an instant. If the protected light goes out, the strip shows heat damage, or it took a major hit, replace it right away regardless of its age.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power strip the same as a surge protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A basic power strip only adds outlets and usually a circuit breaker; it has no surge protection. A surge protector contains components, usually metal oxide varistors, that divert voltage spikes. A real surge protector lists a joule rating, a voltage protection rating and UL 1449 certification on its packaging. If those are missing, treat it as a power strip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a surge protector if I have a whole-house surge protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, ideally. A whole-house unit at the panel stops large surges from the utility line, but it cannot catch surges created inside the home by appliances, and it usually clamps at a higher voltage. A plug-in surge protector at each TV or computer adds a second layer. Electricians recommend using both together for the best protection of expensive electronics.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a surge protector protect against power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A surge protector cuts out the moment power fails and cannot keep anything running. It does help when the power returns, since restoration can cause spikes. To keep a computer, modem or router on through a short outage, you need a UPS battery backup, which includes surge-protected outlets alongside battery-backed ones. For longer outages, a generator or power station is the answer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
